{"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol"]}],"kind":"project","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"hierarchy":{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents","modules":[{"projects":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Create-the-system-under-test","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Verify-the-interaction","kind":"task"}]}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Getting-Started"},{"projects":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Match-any-or-exactly-one","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Capture-and-inspect-arguments","kind":"task"}]}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Argument-Matching"},{"projects":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Return-computed-values","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Order-your-stubs","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Understand-matcher-precedence","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Run-side-effects-with-do","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Drive-callback-based-APIs","kind":"task"}]},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Assert-call-counts","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-order-across-mocks","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-thrown-errors","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Wait-for-calls-that-havent-happened-yet","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-property-reads-and-writes","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Debug-failing-verifications","kind":"task"}]}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification"},{"projects":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Control-what-unstubbed-requirements-return","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Mock-static-requirements-safely","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Use-the-rest-of-Swift-Testings-vocabulary","kind":"task"}]}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Swift-Testing-Integration"}],"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents","doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/$volume","doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Getting-Started"]]},"sections":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"text":" that depends on a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Along the way you’ll meet the two verbs that power every SwiftMocking test: ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"when","type":"text"}]},{"text":" to arrange behavior, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"verify","type":"text"}]},{"text":" to assert it happened.","type":"text"}]}],"backgroundImage":"intro-first-mock.png","kind":"hero","estimatedTimeInMinutes":10,"image":"intro-first-mock.png","title":"Mock Your First Protocol","chapter":"Getting Started"},{"tasks":[{"anchor":"Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","stepsSection":[{"media":null,"code":"01-1-1-pricing-service.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Consider a store that needs prices. Pricing rules live behind a protocol so tests never depend on real pricing logic. Create ","type":"text"},{"code":"PricingService.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with the protocol:","type":"text"}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-1-2-pricing-service.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Now make it mockable: import ","type":"text"},{"code":"SwiftMocking","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and annotate the protocol with ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":":","type":"text"}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-1-3-generated-mock.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"That single annotation is all the macro needs. It generates ","type":"text"},{"code":"MockPricingService","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" next to your protocol — the equivalent of writing this by hand. You never create or edit this file yourself:","type":"text"}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Notice the generated class carries ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"two overloads"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" of ","type":"text"},{"code":"price(for:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". The ","type":"text"},{"code":"ArgMatcher<String>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" version returns an ","type":"text"},{"code":"Interaction","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — calling it, as in ","type":"text"},{"code":"mock.price(for: .any)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"describes"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" a call for stubbing or verification and never runs your code. The plain ","type":"text"},{"code":"String","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" version is the real protocol requirement: calling it records the invocation and returns the stubbed value. That duality is the whole trick — the same syntax describes a call and performs it.","type":"text"}]}],"caption":[]}],"title":"Mark a protocol as mockable","contentSection":[{"media":"chapter-getting-started.png","mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every mock starts life as a plain protocol describing a dependency. The ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro expands that protocol into a ready-to-use mock class at compile time, so the protocol remains your single source of truth — when it changes, the mock changes with it.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"contentAndMedia"}]},{"anchor":"Create-the-system-under-test","stepsSection":[{"media":null,"code":"01-2-1-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Create "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.swift"},{"type":"text","text":". Start with the state the store owns: the items it has registered and the price it recorded for each."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-2-2-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Inject the dependency. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" holds "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"any PricingService"},{"type":"text","text":" rather than a concrete type — that existential is what lets a mock stand in for the real implementation without "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" knowing the difference."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-2-3-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Add the behavior under test. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"register(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" appends the item and asks the pricing service what it costs — the single call your test will stub and then verify."}]}],"caption":[]}],"title":"Create the system under test","contentSection":[{"media":"intro-first-mock.png","mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With the mock generated, build the class you actually want to test. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" takes any "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"type":"text","text":" — in production a real implementation, in tests the mock."}]}],"kind":"contentAndMedia"}]},{"anchor":"Stub-return-values-with-when","stepsSection":[{"media":null,"code":"01-3-1-store-tests.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Create "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"StoreTests.swift"},{"type":"text","text":" and wire up the two objects: the mock, and the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" that receives it. Nothing is stubbed yet — the mock exists, but it has no answers."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-3-2-store-tests.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Arrange the behavior. Each "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"type":"text","text":" describes a call — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"price(for: \"apple\")"},{"type":"text","text":" — and pins the value it returns. The bare string literal is sugar for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".equal(\"apple\")"},{"type":"text","text":", so each stub answers only its own item."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":"01-3-3-store-tests.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Act, then assert. Registering both items drives the real "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" logic, and the prices it recorded are exactly the ones you stubbed."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Run the test. The store called "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"price(for:)"},{"type":"text","text":" twice — once per registered item — and each call was answered by the matching stub. No real pricing logic was ever involved."}]}],"caption":[]}],"title":"Stub return values with when","contentSection":[{"media":"chapter-getting-started.png","mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An unstubbed method can’t answer on its own. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"type":"text","text":" arranges behavior: you describe the call with a matcher — here the literal "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"apple\""},{"type":"text","text":" — and declare what it returns."}]}],"kind":"contentAndMedia"}]},{"anchor":"Verify-the-interaction","stepsSection":[{"media":null,"code":"01-4-1-store-tests.swift","runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Add a verification to the test. The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".any"},{"type":"text","text":" matcher matches every call regardless of the item, and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".called(2)"},{"type":"text","text":" asserts exactly two invocations:"}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Run the test again — it still passes, and now it also fails loudly if the store ever stops consulting the pricing service."}]}],"caption":[]},{"media":null,"code":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirm the assertion has teeth. Temporarily change "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".called(2)"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".called(3)"},{"type":"text","text":" and the test fails at that line with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Unfulfilled call count. Actual: 2"},{"type":"text","text":" — the message reports what really happened, not just that something went wrong. Change it back before moving on."}]}],"caption":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You’ve completed the full mocking loop: "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"generate","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"arrange","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"act","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" on your type, "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"assert","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verify"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}],"title":"Verify the interaction","contentSection":[{"media":"intro-verifying.png","mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Asserting output is half the story; interaction tests also assert "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"that a dependency was used, and how","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verify"},{"type":"text","text":" takes the same call description as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"type":"text","text":" and asserts how often it happened."}]}],"kind":"contentAndMedia"}]}],"kind":"tasks"},{"featuredEyebrow":"Tutorial","abstract":[{"text":"Stubbing ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"apple\""},{"text":" and verifying ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".any"},{"text":" is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"any","type":"text"}]},{"text":" amount, amounts ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"under","type":"text"}]},{"text":" 100, codes ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"in","type":"text"}]},{"text":" a range, collections ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"of a certain size","type":"text"}]},{"text":" — or some predicate only you can express.","type":"text"}],"kind":"callToAction","title":"Match Arguments Precisely","action":{"overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"text":"Get started","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments","isActive":true,"overridingTitle":"Get started","type":"reference"}}],"metadata":{"category":"SwiftMocking","role":"project","categoryPathComponent":"Table-Of-Contents","title":"Mock Your First Protocol"},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol"},"references":{"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/testing-integration#Use-the-rest-of-Swift-Testings-vocabulary":{"kind":"section","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/testing-integration#Use-the-rest-of-Swift-Testings-vocabulary","type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"Every test so far has been an ordinary ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Test","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". This page covers what Swift Testing adds on top: ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"traits"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":", which scope behavior to a test or a whole suite.","type":"text"}],"title":"Use the rest of Swift Testing's vocabulary","role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Use-the-rest-of-Swift-Testings-vocabulary"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/verifying-interactions#Verify-property-reads-and-writes":{"type":"section","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"You’ve verified counts and matched arguments. This page completes the toolkit: counting calls with the full matcher vocabulary, asserting a call happened "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"never"}]},{"type":"text","text":", proving a mock was "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"untouched"}]},{"type":"text","text":", verifying thrown errors, waiting for calls that arrive from another task, distinguishing property reads from writes, and — the signature feature — verifying the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"order"}]},{"type":"text","text":" of calls across multiple mocks."}],"title":"Verify property reads and writes","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-property-reads-and-writes","kind":"section","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-property-reads-and-writes","role":"pseudoSymbol"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/verifying-interactions#Verify-order-across-mocks":{"abstract":[{"text":"You’ve verified counts and matched arguments. This page completes the toolkit: counting calls with the full matcher vocabulary, asserting a call happened ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"never"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", proving a mock was ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"untouched"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", verifying thrown errors, waiting for calls that arrive from another task, distinguishing property reads from writes, and — the signature feature — verifying the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"order"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" of calls across multiple mocks.","type":"text"}],"role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-order-across-mocks","type":"section","kind":"section","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-order-across-mocks","title":"Verify order across mocks"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/verifying-interactions#Assert-call-counts":{"abstract":[{"text":"You’ve verified counts and matched arguments. This page completes the toolkit: counting calls with the full matcher vocabulary, asserting a call happened ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"never"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", proving a mock was ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"untouched"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", verifying thrown errors, waiting for calls that arrive from another task, distinguishing property reads from writes, and — the signature feature — verifying the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"order"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" of calls across multiple mocks.","type":"text"}],"role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Assert-call-counts","type":"section","kind":"section","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/verifying-interactions#Assert-call-counts","title":"Assert call counts"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/matching-arguments":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Stubbing "},{"code":"\"apple\"","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and verifying "},{"code":".any","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"any","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" amount, amounts "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"under"}]},{"text":" 100, codes ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"in"}]},{"type":"text","text":" a range, collections "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"of a certain size"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" — or some predicate only you can express.","type":"text"}],"title":"Match Arguments Precisely","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/matching-arguments","kind":"project","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments","estimatedTime":"10min","role":"project"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/Table-Of-Contents/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification":{"abstract":[],"role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification","type":"topic","kind":"article","url":"\/tutorials\/table-of-contents\/advanced-stubbing-and-verification","title":"Advanced Stubbing and Verification"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/Table-Of-Contents":{"kind":"overview","type":"topic","url":"\/tutorials\/table-of-contents","role":"overview","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents","abstract":[{"text":"Stop hand-writing mock classes. With SwiftMocking, one ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"text":" annotation turns any protocol into a fully featured mock: stub return values, match arguments with expressive matchers, drive callbacks, and verify every interaction — all type-safe, all local, no code generation steps in your build.","type":"text"}],"title":"Meet SwiftMocking"},"01-2-2-store.swift":{"highlights":[{"line":4},{"line":5},{"line":6},{"line":7},{"line":8}],"identifier":"01-2-2-store.swift","syntax":"swift","fileName":"Store.swift","type":"file","content":["class Store {","    var items: [String] = []","    var prices: [String: Int] = [:]","    let pricingService: any PricingService","","    init(pricingService: any PricingService) {","        self.pricingService = pricingService","    }"],"fileType":"swift"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/Table-Of-Contents/Getting-Started":{"abstract":[],"role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Getting-Started","type":"topic","kind":"article","url":"\/tutorials\/table-of-contents\/getting-started","title":"Getting Started"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/dynamic-stubbing#Understand-matcher-precedence":{"kind":"section","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Understand-matcher-precedence","type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"Fixed return values only go so far. Real dependencies ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"compute"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":": a calculator multiplies its operands, a sync engine mutates state, a network service delivers results through callbacks.","type":"text"}],"title":"Understand matcher precedence","role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Understand-matcher-precedence"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/dynamic-stubbing#Order-your-stubs":{"type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"Fixed return values only go so far. Real dependencies ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"compute"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":": a calculator multiplies its operands, a sync engine mutates state, a network service delivers results through callbacks.","type":"text"}],"title":"Order your stubs","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Order-your-stubs","kind":"section","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Order-your-stubs","role":"pseudoSymbol"},"01-1-1-pricing-service.swift":{"highlights":[],"fileType":"swift","fileName":"PricingService.swift","type":"file","content":["protocol PricingService {","    func price(for item: String) -> Int","}"],"identifier":"01-1-1-pricing-service.swift","syntax":"swift"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges":{"type":"section","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Stubbing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"apple\""},{"text":" and verifying ","type":"text"},{"code":".any","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"any"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" amount, amounts ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"under"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" 100, codes ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"in","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" a range, collections ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"of a certain size"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" — or some predicate only you can express.","type":"text"}],"title":"Match with predicates and ranges","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges","kind":"section","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges","role":"pseudoSymbol"},"01-2-1-store.swift":{"identifier":"01-2-1-store.swift","syntax":"swift","fileType":"swift","content":["class Store {","    var items: [String] = []","    var prices: [String: Int] = [:]"],"fileName":"Store.swift","highlights":[],"type":"file"},"01-3-3-store-tests.swift":{"highlights":[{"line":13},{"line":14},{"line":15},{"line":16},{"line":17},{"line":18}],"fileType":"swift","fileName":"StoreTests.swift","type":"file","content":["import SwiftMocking","import Testing","","@Suite","struct StoreTests {","    @Test","    func registerUsesStubbedPrices() {","        let mock = MockPricingService()","        let store = Store(pricingService: mock)","","        when(mock.price(for: \"apple\")).thenReturn(13)","        when(mock.price(for: \"banana\")).thenReturn(17)","","        store.register(\"apple\")","        store.register(\"banana\")","","        #expect(store.prices[\"apple\"] == 13)","        #expect(store.prices[\"banana\"] == 17)"],"identifier":"01-3-3-store-tests.swift","syntax":"swift"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when":{"kind":"section","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when","type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a ","type":"text"},{"code":"Store","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" that depends on a ","type":"text"},{"code":"PricingService","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it.","type":"text"}],"title":"Stub return values with when","role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when"},"chapter-getting-started.png":{"alt":"A when statement routing two different item names to two different return values.","variants":[{"url":"\/images\/SwiftMocking\/chapter-getting-started.png","traits":["1x","light"]}],"type":"image","identifier":"chapter-getting-started.png"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/testing-integration#Mock-static-requirements-safely":{"abstract":[{"text":"Every test so far has been an ordinary ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Test","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". This page covers what 